    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the concept.\n\n**Critical Rendering Path** concerns the browser rendering pipeline. Explain what stage it belongs to, what causes work at that stage, how it can block or delay pixels, and how to measure the bottleneck before changing code.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nIdentify the user-visible symptom, locate the browser/network/build bottleneck, change one variable, measure before and after, and keep the optimization only if it improves the target outcome.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example makes the important logic explicit so you can explain the data flow and complexity during an interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface Timing {\n  start: number;\n  end: number;\n}\n\nfunction duration(\n  timing: Timing,\n): number {\n  return timing.end - timing.start;\n}\n\nconst ms = duration({\n  start: 100,\n  end: 145,\n});\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse the native/browser helper when it communicates the same intent more clearly and does not hide the key performance trade-off.\n\n```ts\nconst start = performance.now();\nconst end = performance.now();\n\nconst ms = end - start;\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse browser DevTools traces to locate the Critical Rendering Path bottleneck, then reduce the amount or frequency of work instead of blindly changing rendering code.\n\nStep 6 — Complexity and measurement:\nState the dominant time/space or network cost, then validate it with profiling, field data, network traces, build analysis, or browser timing tools as appropriate.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases:\nTest slow networks, low-end devices, large datasets, cache misses, repeated interaction, cold loads, and regression after new dependencies or features.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the bottleneck, the measurement, the chosen optimization, the cost/trade-off, and the guardrail that prevents the regression from returning.",
